## Test Case Specification (from test-case-designer)
{step:design}

The test cases are already designed. Your job now is purely engineering planning — do NOT redesign test points. Produce a structured implementation plan for the api-test-author:
1. File-by-file artifact plan: which files under `.pi/api-tests/` must be created or modified (runner.ts, lib/* incl. route-coverage.ts + failure.ts, types/*, prep.ts, coverage-check.ts, cases/*, scenarios/*, baselines/*, coverage-allowlist.json if some route genuinely needs exemption).
2. Map every designed case/scenario step to a concrete target file and its single exported entry function (e.g. `cases/task_create.test.ts` → `runTaskCreateCases`); each `*.test.ts` must export exactly ONE `run*` function, and the runner auto-discovers files (never a hand-maintained suite list).
3. TypeScript contract derivations: for each endpoint in the design, the request DTO and response interface to define in `types/`.
4. The `.pi/api-tests/prep.ts` runtime resolution plan (reuse the inputs report).
5. For baseline/diff cases: exact `ignoreKeys` paths and baseline file names.
6. Reverse-order teardown and cleanup strategy using `try ... finally` and `ctx.registerTeardown`.
7. Incremental-change strategy: if `.pi/api-tests/` already exists, specify per-file deltas (add/modify/keep) instead of a rewrite.

Strict compactness rule: Format as structured Markdown tables; keep under 80 lines to prevent author token saturation.

