## Authored Suite
{step:author}

Run the test suite (`bun run .pi/api-tests/runner.ts`, `npx tsx .pi/api-tests/runner.ts`, or `bash .pi/api-tests/run-api-tests.sh`) and report, per test point / scenario step, which layers passed or failed (if the suite aborts in the prerequisite phase, report exactly which prerequisite could not be resolved and from where it was supposed to come):
- L1 status mismatch (highlighting unexpected 4xx/5xx in red)
- L2 contract violation (name the violated field/type)
- L3 baseline / dual-service diff drift (quote normalized diff)
- L4 semantic & state machine anti-drift assertion
- Summary report verification: check that `.pi/api-tests/reports/latest-summary.md` and `.pi/api-tests/reports/route-coverage.json` were generated, that the summary contains both the case table and the runtime route-coverage table, and that every failed case carries a machine-readable failure code (L1_STATUS_MISMATCH / L2_CONTRACT_VIOLATION / L3_* / L4_SEMANTIC_MISMATCH / PREREQ_UNRESOLVED).
- Route coverage reality check: compare `route-coverage.json` routes against the changed-route inventory from the recon report; a changed route with zero real calls is a coverage defect, not a pass.

Classify every failure as a real contract/assertion defect, an unexpected data drift, or an environment problem (service down, wrong base URL, proxy interference, rejected credential). Credentials always masked. End with exactly one line: `GATE_STATUS: PASS` or `GATE_STATUS: FAIL`.

The orchestrator deterministically re-runs that same command afterwards; a failing command triggers a repair pass back to the test author.
